# pkg-config metadata for mpif, generated by CMake from cmake/mpif.pc.in.
#
# The third way to consume an installed mpif, beside find_package(mpif)
# (cmake/mpifConfig.cmake.in) and bin/mpifort (bin/mpifort.in). This file and
# the wrapper publish one flag inventory through two mechanisms; every value
# either side bakes in comes from the same CMake variable, so what can drift is
# the *shape* of the two lines, and ci-scripts/check-pkg-config.sh compares
# them.
#
# No `Requires:`/`Requires.private:` naming the MPI, and no `Libs.private:` at
# all. libmpif links no MPI -- its MPI_*/PMPI_* references stay undefined and
# the application supplies -lmpi_abi, which MPI-5.0 section 20.2.1 makes "the
# sole direct dependency of the application binary" -- so the ABI library
# belongs in `Libs`, in the static build as much as the shared one, and
# `--libs --static` is `--libs`. It could not be a `Requires:` in any case: the
# MPIs mpif is tested against have their own .pc files pruned away
# (ci-scripts/mpich-prune.txt, ci-scripts/openmpi-prune.txt), so the one
# environment that would exercise such a line is the one where it cannot
# resolve.
#
# No Fortran flags in `Cflags`; they are in `fortran_flags` below. pkg-config
# has one compile-flag field for every language, and the two build systems that
# consume a .pc as a target -- CMake's pkg_check_modules(IMPORTED_TARGET) and
# Meson's dependency() -- put it into unguarded interface compile options, which
# reach a target's C sources too. That is the defect test/CMakeLists.txt works
# around for FindMPI, and it cost a FreeBSD CI leg: clang errors on
# -fallow-argument-mismatch where gcc's C frontend only warns. A Fortran
# consumer asks for both:
#
#     $FC $(pkg-config --cflags mpif) \
#         $(pkg-config --variable=fortran_flags mpif) -c app.f90
#
# `mpi_prefix` and `mpi_libdir` are the pkg-config spelling of the wrapper's
# MPIF_MPI_PREFIX and MPIF_MPI_LIBDIR. The MPI recorded here is only a
# *default*:
#
#     pkg-config --define-variable=mpi_prefix=<other-prefix> --libs mpif
#
# links another one, and the loader's search path substitutes a third at run
# time, same binary, no relink. As in the wrapper, `mpi_libdir` is written
# relative to `mpi_prefix` when the MPI's libdir is under its prefix, and
# absolutely when it is not -- in which case redirect `mpi_libdir` itself.
#
# pkg-config expands ${...} in these values and treats # as starting a comment,
# so a literal $ in CMAKE_Fortran_FLAGS (say -Wl,-rpath,'$ORIGIN') has to be
# written $$, and a # would silently truncate the line. Measured on pkg-config
# 0.29.2. The wrapper has no such exposure; this is the one asymmetry between
# the two templates.

prefix=@CMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX@
exec_prefix=${prefix}
libdir=@MPIF_PC_LIBDIR@
includedir=@MPIF_PC_INCLUDEDIR@
mpi_prefix=@MPIF_MPI_PREFIX@
mpi_libdir=@MPIF_PC_MPI_LIBDIR@

# The Fortran compiler that built this mpif, recorded for the reason
# cmake/mpifConfig.cmake.in records it: the installed mpi.mod and mpi_f08.mod
# are in that compiler's private module format, and a consumer using another one
# has its first `use mpi_f08` rejected by whatever its own compiler says about a
# module it cannot read. A .pc file cannot compare and warn the way the CMake
# config file does, so this is the value and nothing more -- read it with
# `pkg-config --variable=fortran_compiler_id mpif`.
# ci-scripts/check-pkg-config.sh compiles with `fortran_compiler`, which is what
# keeps these three from being decoration.
fortran_compiler=@CMAKE_Fortran_COMPILER@
fortran_compiler_id=@CMAKE_Fortran_COMPILER_ID@
fortran_compiler_version=@CMAKE_Fortran_COMPILER_VERSION@

# Character for character the wrapper's MPIF_FCFLAGS default: today
# -fallow-argument-mismatch where the compiler takes it, which include/mpif.h
# genuinely needs (see MPIF_Fortran_FLAGS_LIST in CMakeLists.txt), plus whatever
# CMAKE_Fortran_FLAGS the build was given.
fortran_flags=@CMAKE_Fortran_FLAGS@ @MPIF_Fortran_FLAGS_STR@

Name: @PROJECT_NAME@
Description: @PROJECT_DESCRIPTION@
URL: @CMAKE_PROJECT_HOMEPAGE_URL@
Version: @PROJECT_VERSION@
Cflags: -I${includedir}
# The rpaths are here on purpose. mpif's whole run-time arrangement rests on
# DT_RUNPATH: the executable's sole direct MPI dependency is the ABI library's
# conventional versioned name, found through the runpath baked here, which
# LD_LIBRARY_PATH precedes -- MPIF_LINKER_DTAGS_FLAG (-Wl,--enable-new-dtags on
# ELF) is what makes it RUNPATH rather than RPATH, which would shadow the
# override. Without them a pkg-config-built binary links and then dies at exec,
# so this route would have no default MPI at all, unlike the other two. A
# packager who does not want them (a prefix of /usr) must edit this file:
# pkg-config strips -L/usr/lib but nothing strips -Wl,-rpath,/usr/lib, and
# `--define-variable=x=` with an empty value is a hard error, so no knob could
# have been offered here.
#
# The dtags flag and the sanitizer string are substituted with nothing between
# them because MPIF_SANITIZE_LINK_STR carries its own leading space (it is built
# with string(APPEND ... " ...")), and it is empty in an ordinary build. When it
# is not, it precedes -lmpif and -lmpi_abi for the reason bin/mpifort.in gives:
# DT_NEEDED follows link order and ASan refuses to run unless its runtime leads
# the process's initial library list. Measured on pkg-config 0.29.2: every -L is
# hoisted to the front of --libs, but the order of -l fragments and of
# everything else -- -Wl,* and the absolute runtime path the sanitizer string
# names -- is preserved relative to each other, so the ordering that matters
# survives. That hoisting is also why ci-scripts/check-pkg-config.sh compares
# this line against the wrapper's as a set and not a sequence.
Libs: @LINK_FLAGS@ @MPIF_LINKER_DTAGS_FLAG@@MPIF_SANITIZE_LINK_STR@ -L${libdir} -Wl,-rpath,${libdir} -lmpif -L${mpi_libdir} -Wl,-rpath,${mpi_libdir} -lmpi_abi
